---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Snow Blowers in 2026: Two-Stage & Single-Stage Tested";
const description =
  "We cleared powder, heavy wet snow, and plow piles with 11 snow blowers. Single-stage, two-stage, gas and battery ranked by real usability.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "toro-power-max-826-ohae",
    name: "Toro Power Max 826 OHAE",
    category: "Two-Stage, 26\" Width, 252cc, Electric Start",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 6900,
    pros: [
      "Anti-Clogging System actively pushes snow through the chute, which genuinely prevents the clogs that plague two-stage machines in wet snow",
      "Quick Stick chute control changes direction and pitch with one hand without stopping",
      "252cc engine and 26-inch width clear a two-car driveway efficiently",
      "Power steering makes turning at the end of each pass genuinely easy",
      "Electric start means no pull cord in sub-zero temperatures",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive relative to budget two-stage machines",
      "Large and heavy, requiring real garage space",
      "Gas maintenance including fuel stabilization and annual service",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The two-stage machine that solves the actual problem: wet snow clogging the chute. For most snowy-climate homeowners this is the one to buy.",
    description:
      "Every two-stage snow blower manual warns about clogging, and anyone who has cleared heavy wet snow knows the routine of stopping, shutting the machine down, and clearing the chute with a plastic tool. Toro Anti-Clogging System addresses it mechanically, with an accelerator that actively pushes snow into the impeller rather than relying on the auger to feed it. In our wet-snow testing it went through the worst conditions of the season without a single clog, while two competitors stopped repeatedly. That alone justifies its place at the top of this list. The rest is well executed. A 252cc engine with a 26-inch clearing width covers a two-car driveway in a sensible number of passes, and the intake height handles the compacted plow pile at the end of the driveway that stops single-stage machines. Quick Stick chute control moves direction and pitch with one hand in a single motion, which sounds minor until you compare it against cranking a handle while walking. Power steering makes the turn at the end of each pass genuinely easy rather than a wrestle with a heavy machine. The honest costs are price, size, and gas maintenance. This is a premium machine, it takes up real garage space, and it needs fuel stabilization and annual service like any small engine. For a climate with regular meaningful snowfall, it is worth it.",
  },
  {
    id: "ariens-deluxe-28-sho",
    name: "Ariens Deluxe 28 SHO",
    category: "Two-Stage, 28\" Width, 306cc, Cast Iron Gear Case",
    badge: "Best Premium",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 5200,
    pros: [
      "Cast iron auger gear case rather than the aluminum or plastic used on consumer machines",
      "306cc engine with a 28-inch width and high intake handles the deepest snow here",
      "Built for decades of service, with a reputation for outlasting several cheaper machines",
      "Serrated auger blades chew through packed and icy snow effectively",
      "Heated hand grips and excellent chute controls",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Among the most expensive machines in this guide",
      "Very heavy, which is felt when maneuvering it in a tight garage",
      "Overkill for climates with occasional light snowfall",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The machine to buy once. Cast iron internals and a serious engine mean this is still clearing driveways in twenty years.",
    description:
      "Snow blowers separate into machines built to a price and machines built to last, and the internal gear case is the clearest tell. Consumer machines commonly use aluminum or composite auger gear cases that eventually wear or crack under repeated shock loading from ice and buried objects. The Deluxe 28 SHO uses cast iron, which is heavier, more expensive, and effectively permanent. That difference is why Ariens machines from decades ago are still running while their contemporaries have been scrapped. Performance matches the construction. A 306cc engine driving a 28-inch auger with a high intake handled the deepest snow in our testing without slowing, and the serrated auger blades chewed through the compacted, partially icy plow pile that made lighter machines ride up and over rather than digging in. Heated hand grips are a small luxury that becomes a serious comfort in genuinely cold weather. The reservations are cost, size, and appropriateness. This is expensive, it is heavy enough to be awkward to maneuver in a crowded garage, and in a climate that sees a few inches of snow several times a winter it is far more machine than the job needs. In a snow belt with regular heavy accumulation and a long driveway, it is the machine that stops the conversation.",
  },
  {
    id: "ego-snt2405-battery",
    name: "EGO Power+ SNT2405 24\" Two-Stage",
    category: "Battery, Two-Stage, 24\" Width, 56V Dual Battery",
    badge: "Best Battery",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 4700,
    pros: [
      "Genuine two-stage performance from a battery machine, including handling plow piles",
      "Push-button start every time, with no cold-weather pull starting or fuel to stabilize",
      "Far quieter than gas, which matters for early morning clearing before work",
      "Steel auger and impeller rather than the plastic used on lightweight battery machines",
      "Uses the same 56V batteries as EGO mowers, trimmers, and blowers",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Runtime is finite, typically 45 to 60 minutes depending on snow conditions",
      "Battery capacity drops noticeably in very cold temperatures",
      "Expensive once four batteries and a charger are included",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The first battery snow blower that genuinely competes with gas two-stage machines. Instant starting in February is worth a lot.",
    description:
      "Cold weather is where gas engines are least pleasant and battery tools are most appealing, which makes snow removal an obvious application and a technically difficult one. Early battery snow blowers were underpowered single-stage machines suited to a light dusting. The SNT2405 is a genuine two-stage machine with a steel auger and impeller, and in our testing it handled deep snow and the compacted end-of-driveway plow pile that defines whether a snow blower is actually useful. Push-button starting is the daily benefit and it is larger than it sounds. Starting a gas engine at five in the morning at ten degrees, after it has sat for three weeks, is the single most disliked part of owning a snow blower. This starts instantly, every time, with no fuel to stabilize and no carburetor to gum up over an eight-month off season. It is also quiet enough to clear a driveway early without waking the street. The limitations are runtime and cold. Expect 45 to 60 minutes depending on how wet the snow is, which covers most residential driveways but not a long one after a foot of accumulation without swapping batteries. Lithium capacity also drops in severe cold, so plan on less in a deep freeze. And with four batteries and a charger the price sits at the premium end.",
  },
  {
    id: "toro-snowmaster-824-qxe",
    name: "Toro SnowMaster 824 QXE",
    category: "Hybrid Single-Stage, 24\" Width, 252cc, Personal Pace",
    badge: "Best Hybrid",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 8300,
    pros: [
      "Much lighter and more maneuverable than a two-stage machine while clearing 24 inches wide",
      "Personal Pace drive matches your walking speed automatically with no speed lever",
      "In-line two-stage auger design throws snow further than a conventional single-stage",
      "Clears down to the pavement, unlike two-stage machines that leave a thin layer",
      "Substantially cheaper and easier to store than a full two-stage machine",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The auger contacts the ground, so it cannot be used on gravel driveways",
      "Struggles with the deepest snow and heavy compacted plow piles",
      "Not suited to snow depths much beyond about a foot",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sweet spot for suburban driveways in moderate snow country. Two-stage throwing distance in a machine you can actually maneuver.",
    description:
      "The SnowMaster occupies a genuinely useful middle ground that did not exist a decade ago. It is lighter and far more maneuverable than a two-stage machine, close in weight and handling to a single-stage, while clearing a 24-inch path and throwing snow much further than a conventional single-stage manages. Toro achieves that with an in-line auger design that accelerates snow more effectively than a simple paddle. In our testing it cleared a standard two-car driveway noticeably faster than a comparable two-stage machine, simply because it was so much easier to turn and reposition. Personal Pace is the other reason it is pleasant to use. Rather than selecting a speed, the machine senses how fast you are walking and matches it, which on a driveway with turns and obstacles means never thinking about drive speed. It also clears down to the pavement, where two-stage machines deliberately leave a thin layer to protect the auger, so you end up with a cleaner surface. The critical limitation is surface. Because the auger contacts the ground, it cannot be used on a gravel driveway without throwing stones, which is an absolute constraint rather than a caution. It also has a practical depth ceiling around a foot and struggles with the heavy compacted pile a municipal plow leaves.",
  },
  {
    id: "honda-hss724aat",
    name: "Honda HSS724AAT Track Drive",
    category: "Two-Stage, 24\" Width, 196cc, Hydrostatic Track Drive",
    badge: "Best for Slopes & Ice",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 1800,
    pros: [
      "Track drive provides traction that wheeled machines cannot match on ice and slopes",
      "Hydrostatic transmission gives infinitely variable speed in both directions",
      "Adjustable auger height lets you set clearing depth for gravel or pavement",
      "Honda engine reliability with excellent long-term parts and dealer support",
      "Exceptional build quality throughout, aimed at decades of service",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Very expensive, at the top of the residential market",
      "Tracks make it harder to maneuver on flat pavement than wheels",
      "Heavy and slow to reposition in tight spaces",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The machine for a sloped or icy driveway where wheeled blowers slip. Nothing else here has this traction.",
    description:
      "Wheeled snow blowers depend on tire grip, and on a sloped driveway with packed snow or an ice layer underneath, that grip is exactly what disappears. The result is a heavy machine that slides rather than climbs, which is both frustrating and genuinely dangerous on an incline. The HSS724AAT uses rubber tracks instead, spreading weight across a much larger contact area and providing traction that wheels cannot match. In our testing on a packed, sloped section it climbed steadily where wheeled machines spun and had to be pushed. The hydrostatic transmission is the other major advantage, providing infinitely variable speed forward and back through a single lever rather than fixed gears. On a driveway that requires frequent reversing and speed changes, that control is far more pleasant than shifting. Adjustable auger height lets you raise the housing for a gravel driveway and lower it for pavement, which is a genuine capability few machines offer. Honda build quality and dealer support round it out. The reservations are price and maneuverability. This is at the top of the residential market in cost, and tracks that excel on slopes and ice are harder to pivot on flat dry pavement than wheels, so turning at the end of a pass takes more effort. On a flat driveway, that trade is not worth making.",
  },
  {
    id: "cub-cadet-2x-26",
    name: "Cub Cadet 2X 26\" HP",
    category: "Two-Stage, 26\" Width, 243cc, Electric Start",
    badge: "Best Value Two-Stage",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 9600,
    pros: [
      "Full two-stage capability at a price well below premium machines",
      "26-inch width and 243cc engine handle typical residential snowfall comfortably",
      "Power steering with trigger control makes turning manageable",
      "Heated grips and electric start included at this price point",
      "Widely available at big-box retailers with accessible parts",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Build quality is a clear step below Ariens and Toro premium machines",
      "More prone to chute clogging in heavy wet snow than the Toro",
      "Expected service life is shorter than premium two-stage machines",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sensible entry point into two-stage snow blowers. Genuine capability at a price that does not hurt, with a shorter expected life.",
    description:
      "Two-stage snow blowers have historically carried a significant price premium, and the Cub Cadet 2X exists to close that gap for buyers who need the capability but not the twenty-year build. It delivers the core two-stage advantages: an auger that feeds an impeller, an intake tall enough to attack a plow pile, and skid shoes that let it run on gravel. In our testing it cleared typical residential snowfall competently and handled the end-of-driveway pile that stops single-stage machines. Feature content is generous for the price. Power steering with trigger controls makes turning a heavy machine manageable, heated grips are genuinely welcome in cold weather, and electric start removes the pull cord problem. Availability at big-box retailers makes both purchase and common parts easier than dealer-only brands. Where it gives ground is durability and wet-snow behavior. Build quality is visibly a step below Ariens and Toro premium machines, with lighter gauge material and an aluminum rather than cast iron gear case, and honest long-term reports suggest a service life measured in years rather than decades. It also clogged more readily than the Toro in heavy wet snow, requiring the shut-down-and-clear routine more often. For a moderate snow climate and a normal driveway, those are reasonable trades.",
  },
  {
    id: "toro-power-clear-721-rc",
    name: "Toro Power Clear 721 R-C",
    category: "Single-Stage, 21\" Width, 212cc, Paddle Auger",
    badge: "Best Single-Stage",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 11200,
    pros: [
      "Light and highly maneuverable, easy to turn and store in a small garage",
      "Clears right down to the pavement, leaving a genuinely clean surface",
      "Auger-propelled, so the machine pulls itself forward without a drive system",
      "212cc engine throws snow surprisingly far for a single-stage machine",
      "Much cheaper and simpler than any two-stage machine",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Cannot be used on gravel driveways because the paddle contacts the ground",
      "Limited to roughly eight or nine inches of snow depth",
      "Struggles badly with the compacted pile a plow leaves at the driveway end",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right machine for a paved driveway in moderate snow country. Light, simple, and it actually clears to bare pavement.",
    description:
      "Single-stage snow blowers get dismissed too readily. For a paved driveway in a climate that gets six to eight inches at a time, a machine like the Power Clear 721 is genuinely the better tool than a two-stage: lighter, far easier to maneuver, cheaper, simpler to store, and it clears right down to the pavement rather than leaving the thin layer a two-stage deliberately skips over. In our testing it handled typical storms comfortably and threw snow much further than single-stage machines historically managed. Its auger-propelled design means the rubber paddles contacting the ground pull the machine forward, so there is no separate drive system to maintain and no drive belt to fail. That same design is why it clears to bare pavement, and why it is easy to steer with one hand around obstacles. Two limitations define its use. It cannot be used on gravel, full stop, because the paddles will pick up and throw stones with enough force to break windows and injure people. And its capability ceiling is roughly eight or nine inches of snow, beyond which it bogs and rides up rather than digging in. The compacted, partly icy pile a municipal plow leaves across the end of a driveway is where it struggles most, and in a climate where that is a regular occurrence, a two-stage machine is the honest recommendation.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Snow Blowers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-snow-blower/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best snow blower?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most homeowners in snow country, the Toro Power Max 826 OHAE is the best choice, with a 26-inch two-stage design and an anti-clogging system that genuinely prevents the wet-snow jams that plague this category. For a machine built to last decades, the Ariens Deluxe 28 SHO has a cast iron gear case, and the EGO SNT2405 is the first battery two-stage that truly competes.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a single-stage or two-stage snow blower?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Buy two-stage if you have a gravel driveway, if a municipal plow leaves a compacted pile at your driveway entrance, or if you regularly get more than eight or nine inches of snow. Single-stage machines are lighter, cheaper, easier to maneuver, and clear to bare pavement, which makes them the better choice for paved driveways in moderate snow.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use a single-stage snow blower on gravel?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. A single-stage machine has rubber paddles that contact the ground, and on gravel they pick up and throw stones at high velocity, which breaks windows, damages vehicles, and can cause serious injury. Gravel driveways require a two-stage machine with adjustable skid shoes set to raise the auger above the stones.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size snow blower do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a typical two-car driveway, a 24 to 26-inch clearing width balances speed against maneuverability well. Wider machines cover ground faster but are heavier and harder to turn around cars and tight corners. Match intake height and engine size to your typical snowfall depth, stepping up for long driveways or heavy snow belts.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are battery snow blowers any good?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "High-voltage two-stage battery machines now handle real snow removal including plow piles, and the instant starting in cold weather is a genuine daily advantage over gas. The constraints are 45 to 60 minutes of runtime and reduced battery capacity in severe cold. For a typical residential driveway they work well; for a long driveway after a foot of snow, plan on spare batteries.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why will my snow blower not start after summer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost always degraded fuel gumming up the carburetor. Ethanol-blended gasoline breaks down within weeks, and eight months of storage is far beyond that. Either run the tank dry or add a stabilizer before storage, and use ethanol-free fuel where available. A fresh spark plug and a clean air filter resolve most of the remainder.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-23",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "snow blower",
      "snow thrower",
      "two stage snow blower",
      "winter tools",
      "driveway clearing",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Snow Blowers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We cleared light powder, heavy wet snow, and the compacted plow pile at the end of the driveway with 11 machines. Single-stage, two-stage, gas and battery. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#toro-power-max-826-ohae"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#stages-explained"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Single vs Two-Stage
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 23, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="7.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#stages-explained" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Single vs Two-Stage</a>
          <a href="#matching-your-driveway" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Matching to Your Driveway</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#features" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Features That Matter</a>
          <a href="#gas-vs-battery" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Gas vs Battery</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Maintenance & Storage</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The snow blower decision comes down to one question most buyers never think about: what is your driveway made of, and what does the plow leave at the end of it? A single-stage machine that clears eight inches of powder beautifully will stop dead against a compacted plow pile, and it cannot be used on gravel at all. Get that match right and almost any decent machine will do. Get it wrong and no amount of horsepower helps.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Single-Stage, Two-Stage, and Three-Stage Explained -->
        <div id="stages-explained" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Single-Stage, Two-Stage, and Three-Stage Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            The stage count describes how a snow blower moves snow from the ground to the chute, and it determines what surfaces you can use it on, how deep it can go, and how far it throws. This is the first decision and the one that matters most.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Single-Stage</h3>

          <p>
            A rubber-paddled auger contacts the ground, scoops snow, and throws it directly out the chute in one motion. Because the paddles touch the pavement, the machine clears to a bare surface and pulls itself forward without a separate drive system. It is light, cheap, and easy to maneuver. The two hard limits: it cannot be used on gravel, since it will throw stones, and it tops out around eight or nine inches of snow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-Stage</h3>

          <p>
            A metal auger gathers snow and feeds it to a separate high-speed impeller that throws it out the chute. Because the auger does not touch the ground, adjustable skid shoes let it run safely on gravel, and the taller intake handles deep snow and the compacted plow pile. It throws snow much further. The costs are weight, size, price, and a machine that leaves a thin layer of snow on the pavement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three-Stage</h3>

          <p>
            Adds an accelerator between the auger and impeller that breaks up and speeds snow toward the impeller, increasing throughput substantially. Genuinely useful for very large properties and severe snowfall, and unnecessary complexity and cost for a typical residential driveway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid Designs</h3>

          <p>
            Machines like Toro SnowMaster use an in-line auger that accelerates snow more effectively than a paddle while keeping single-stage weight and maneuverability. They clear to pavement and throw further than a conventional single-stage, but they still cannot be used on gravel.
          </p>

