        <div id="features" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Features Worth Paying For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Snow blower feature lists are long, and only a handful of items genuinely change the experience of clearing a driveway at six in the morning in the cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Electric start.</strong> The single most worthwhile feature on any gas snow blower. Pull starting a cold engine that has sat since last February, in sub-zero temperatures with gloves on, is the part of snow blower ownership people hate most. Push-button starting from a wall outlet or an onboard battery removes it entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Chute control quality.</strong> You change chute direction constantly to avoid throwing snow into the wind, onto a car, or into a neighbor driveway. A single-hand joystick or quick-stick control does this without stopping; a crank handle means pausing and cranking repeatedly. Over a season this is one of the biggest differences in how pleasant a machine is.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Power steering or trigger steering.</strong> On a heavy two-stage machine, turning at the end of every pass means muscling a couple hundred pounds around. Trigger-controlled steering disengages one wheel to let the machine pivot, which turns that wrestle into a light movement.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Heated hand grips.</strong> Sounds like a luxury, and in genuinely cold weather it is the difference between finishing the driveway and going inside. Cheap to include, and consistently one of the features owners say they would not go without again.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Headlights.</strong> Snow clearing happens before dawn and after dark more often than not, and a headlight on the machine is far more useful than a porch light behind you casting your own shadow across the work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Anti-clogging systems.</strong> Wet snow clogging the chute is the most common two-stage frustration, and mechanical systems that actively push snow through genuinely reduce it. If you get wet coastal snow rather than dry powder, this is worth prioritizing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Never Clear a Chute With Your Hand</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A clogged chute holds stored energy in the auger and impeller, and clearing it by hand is how people lose fingers, including with the engine switched off, because the tension releases as soon as the obstruction moves. Shut the engine off, wait for everything to stop, and use the clearing tool that comes clipped to the machine. Every snow blower includes one for exactly this reason.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Gas, Battery, or Corded Electric -->
        <div id="gas-vs-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Gas, Battery, or Corded Electric
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power source affects starting reliability, runtime, and how much off-season maintenance the machine demands, and the calculation is different from other outdoor tools because of cold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas</h3>

          <p>
            Still the default for serious snow removal. Unlimited runtime, the highest available power, and the only option in the largest sizes. The costs are real: cold-weather starting, fuel that degrades over an eight-month off season and gums carburetors, annual service, and noise. Electric start mitigates the worst of it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery</h3>

          <p>
            Battery snow blowers have improved dramatically, and high-voltage two-stage machines now handle genuine snow removal including plow piles. The daily benefit is enormous: instant starting in any temperature, no fuel to stabilize, and nothing to gum up over the summer. The constraints are runtime of roughly 45 to 60 minutes and reduced battery capacity in severe cold, which is precisely when you need it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded Electric</h3>

          <p>
            Cheap, light, and effective for a short paved driveway or a set of steps and walkways. The cord is a genuine nuisance in snow, where it drags through what you just cleared and stiffens in the cold. Range is capped around 100 feet from an outlet. For a small paved area it is a sensible budget choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Cold-Weather Battery Caveat</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium batteries lose meaningful capacity in severe cold, so a machine rated for an hour in mild conditions may deliver noticeably less at ten degrees. Storing batteries indoors and installing them just before use recovers most of that, and it is worth building the habit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Maintenance, Storage, and Getting It to Start in December -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Maintenance, Storage, and Getting It to Start in December
          </h2>

          <p>
            Snow blowers sit unused for eight months and are then expected to start in freezing weather on the morning they are needed. Nearly every failure traces to how the machine was put away.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fuel is the enemy.</strong> Ethanol-blended gasoline degrades within weeks and gums up small carburetors, and eight months is a long time. Either run the tank completely dry at the end of the season or fill it and add a fuel stabilizer, and consider ethanol-free fuel for seasonal equipment. This single decision accounts for the large majority of snow blowers that will not start in December.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Change the oil before storage, not after.</strong> Used oil contains combustion acids that corrode internal components while the engine sits. Changing it in spring rather than fall means the engine spends the off season with clean oil in it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the shear pins and keep spares.</strong> Shear pins are designed to break when the auger hits something solid, protecting the gear case. They are cheap, and a snow blower with a broken shear pin will not throw snow. Keep a set in the garage, because they always break during a storm and never when the hardware store is open.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Adjust the skid shoes for your surface.</strong> On pavement, set them so the scraper bar is close to the ground for a clean clear. On gravel, raise them substantially so the auger rides above the stones. Getting this wrong on gravel is how a two-stage machine ends up throwing rocks.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Inspect belts before the season.</strong> Auger and drive belts stretch and crack, and a belt failure mid-storm means the machine stops working when you need it most. Checking them in the fall and keeping a spare is cheap insurance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store it dry and covered.</strong> Snow blowers spend their working life wet, and putting one away damp for eight months rusts the auger housing and chute. Run it briefly after the last use to clear snow from the housing, wipe it down, and consider a light spray of silicone in the chute, which also reduces clogging.
          </p>
        </div>

