          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best snow blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners in snow country, the Toro Power Max 826 OHAE is the best choice, with a 26-inch two-stage design and an anti-clogging system that genuinely prevents the wet-snow jams that plague this category. For a machine built to last decades, the Ariens Deluxe 28 SHO has a cast iron gear case, and the EGO SNT2405 is the first battery two-stage that truly competes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Single-stage or two-stage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy two-stage if you have a gravel driveway, if a municipal plow leaves a compacted pile at your driveway entrance, or if you regularly get more than eight or nine inches of snow. Single-stage machines are lighter, cheaper, easier to maneuver, and clear to bare pavement, which makes them the better choice for paved driveways in moderate snow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use one on gravel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A single-stage machine has rubber paddles that contact the ground, and on gravel they pick up and throw stones at high velocity, which breaks windows, damages vehicles, and can cause serious injury. Gravel driveways require a two-stage machine with adjustable skid shoes set to raise the auger above the stones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical two-car driveway, a 24 to 26-inch clearing width balances speed against maneuverability well. Wider machines cover ground faster but are heavier and harder to turn around cars and tight corners. Match intake height and engine size to your typical snowfall depth, stepping up for long driveways or heavy snow belts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are battery snow blowers any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                High-voltage two-stage battery machines now handle real snow removal including plow piles, and the instant starting in cold weather is a genuine daily advantage over gas. The constraints are 45 to 60 minutes of runtime and reduced battery capacity in severe cold. For a typical residential driveway they work well; for a long driveway after a foot of snow, plan on spare batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why will it not start after summer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always degraded fuel gumming up the carburetor. Ethanol-blended gasoline breaks down within weeks, and eight months of storage is far beyond that. Either run the tank dry or add a stabilizer before storage, and use ethanol-free fuel where available. A fresh spark plug and a clean air filter resolve most of the remainder.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Snow Blower Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Snow blower problems fall into two categories: buying the wrong machine for the driveway, and one habit that causes serious injuries every winter.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Clearing a Clogged Chute by Hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A jammed auger and impeller hold stored energy, and it releases the instant the obstruction moves, even with the engine off. This is how people lose fingers every single winter. Shut the machine down, wait for all rotation to stop, and use the clearing tool clipped to the machine, which every snow blower includes for exactly this purpose.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Single-Stage Machine for a Gravel Driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Single-stage paddles contact the ground and will throw gravel with enough force to break windows and injure people. There is no adjustment or technique that makes this safe. Gravel driveways require a two-stage machine with skid shoes raised to keep the auger above the stones.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing It With Untreated Fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ethanol-blended gasoline degrades in weeks and gums small carburetors, and a snow blower sits for eight months. This is the single largest cause of machines that will not start on the morning of the first storm. Run the tank dry or stabilize the fuel, and change the oil in the fall rather than the spring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating the Plow Pile</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The compacted, partly icy ridge a municipal plow leaves across a driveway entrance is far denser than fallen snow, and it stops single-stage and lightweight machines completely. If your driveway meets a plowed road, that pile is a regular event and it should be the deciding factor in your machine choice, not an afterthought.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

